# seestar-mcp configuration — copy to `.env` and edit.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# `.env` is gitignored and must stay that way: it is the only place a key belongs.
# Every setting here is also settable as a real environment variable, which takes
# precedence over this file. Full list of options: src/seestar_mcp/config.py

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The bridge (seestar_alp) — required
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Where seestar_alp serves its ASCOM Alpaca API. Localhost is correct for the
# normal setup (bridge and MCP server on the same host). Never expose publicly.
SEESTAR_ALPACA_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:5555

# The Alpaca device number seestar_alp registered your scope at. This is the
# number in ITS config.toml, and the shipped example uses 1 — NOT 0. If every
# device call errors while the bridge itself answers, check this first.
SEESTAR_ALPACA_DEVICE_NUM=1

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The scope on your LAN — required
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Your Seestar's IP. Give it a DHCP reservation so it does not move.
SEESTAR_SEESTAR_HOST=192.168.1.50

# Path to the Seestar's MyWorks folder. When set, list_subs / download_subs read
# subs straight off the share instead of over JSON-RPC — this is the validated
# path, and the only one confirmed working on firmware 8.46.
#   Windows: \\<scope-ip>\EMMC Images\MyWorks
#   Linux/macOS: a mount point, e.g. /mnt/seestar/MyWorks
SEESTAR_SEESTAR_IMAGE_ROOT=\\192.168.1.50\EMMC Images\MyWorks

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Weather — optional. THE DEFAULT IS FREE AND NEEDS NO KEY.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Leave the line below commented out and the planner uses **Open-Meteo**:
# keyless, free, no account, no quota. This is the default and it is enough for
# the go/no-go verdict, cloud cover, wind and dew risk. Most users never need
# anything else.
#
# meteoblue is an OPTIONAL upgrade — a multi-model forecast that can be more
# accurate for marginal nights. It is a PAID, credit-metered service.
#
#   READ THIS BEFORE ENABLING IT:
#   Each forecast call costs credits, and a multi-package request is not cheap.
#   On 2026-07-31 a dashboard polled check_night_guardrails once a minute for
#   eleven hours — including all day after the session ended — and burned
#   roughly 8 MILLION credits in a single day. The fix (a 15-minute TTL cache,
#   SEESTAR_WEATHER_CACHE_TTL_S below) ships in this repo and cuts that by ~95%,
#   but you are still paying per fetch. If anything polls this server on a
#   timer, verify the cache is on before you enable a key.
#
# To use meteoblue:
#   1. Sign up at https://www.meteoblue.com/en/weather-api and get an API key.
#   2. Uncomment the line below and paste your key after the `=`.
#   3. Keep it in `.env` only. Never commit it, never paste it into an issue.
#      The provenance log redacts anything matching key/secret/token, so it will
#      not leak there — but the file itself is your responsibility.
#   4. Watch your credit usage for the first night.
#
#SEESTAR_METEOBLUE_API_KEY=

# How long a weather forecast is reused before refetching, in seconds. Applies
# to BOTH providers; it exists because the guardrail consumes only one boolean
# from the whole forecast and used to refetch it on every poll. 0 disables
# caching (not recommended if you use meteoblue).
SEESTAR_WEATHER_CACHE_TTL_S=900

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Notes
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# - No RSA / firmware interop key belongs here. seestar_alp owns that handshake
#   and points at its own PEM; this server never handles it.
# - QA thresholds (SEESTAR_QA_*) are all overridable — see config.py. The
#   defaults are session-relative and suit a Seestar S50; you should not need to
#   touch them to get started.
